  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "vcC+/0aeUziLBCiVkjXa6BAd7b0=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:24.903Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The integration point is a consent event in a Solid pod, dispatched as a typed operation so that there is an append-only record of who consented to what. The record is the history of consent decisions rather than the current state of permissions.\n\nConsent is a natural fit for this record layer because the question asked of it has the same shape as the question asked of an approval chain: who decided, to what, and when. A permission set answers what is allowed now; a data-subject enquiry or a regulator asks what was allowed at a past moment and on what basis, which requires the decisions to have been kept.\n\nTwo Belgian leads carry this demo, Athumi at \"1 \u00b7 parallel track\" and Digita (use.id) at \"2 \u00b7 next\", both from the Benelux-suppliers research pass, with source rows naming Solid alongside Inrupt ESS and use.id. Both being priority 1 or 2 makes this one of the warmest two-lead groups in the slice, and the personal-data-vault context supplies the audit motive without anyone having to construct it.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (2)\n\n**1 \u00b7 parallel track**\n- Athumi (BE)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Digita (use.id) (BE)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Solid pod consent event dispatching a typed operation \u2014 append-only record of who consented to what.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:31:24.903126Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:31:24.903130Z"
    }
  },
